    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a deck build in vancouver. gotta use cedar for the framing. can u explain the difference between live load and dead load for the joist calculations? i want to make sure my spacing is right so nothing fails.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Dead load accounts for the permanent weight of your structure, like the cedar decking, joists, and roofing materials. Live load is the temporary weight that might be placed on the deck, such as people, furniture, or heavy snow. For your calculations, you must add both the expected dead load and the applicable live load to determine the total required load capacity for your joists. This ensures your design is safe under all possible conditions.",
